  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "StandBy on any screen",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"StandBy on any screen\"**\n\nThe desk is an iPhone lying on its side at night: two widgets straight on pure black, no cards, no borders, each owning its half of the screen and one vivid iOS system tint. The first thing anyone sees from across a room is the time, in heavy rounded digits that fill most of the height, then the second widget. Nothing else competes. The controls are not there until someone taps; then a small translucent pill appears top right and the page dots flash, and both fade away again.\n\nThere is one look and no theme picker. The board is ambient and quiet; every screen off the board (settings, the phone remote, the macapad key editor, popups) is plain iOS dark UI, the way Settings, Home and Control Center look: large bold titles, inset grouped lists on #1c1c1e, pill buttons, a blue accent, a green switch. On the Echo Show 5 (960×480) settings is an iPad split view; on the iPhone (393×852) it is a drill-in list. Copy is plain sentence case for ordinary people, never developer syntax.\n\nAfter sunset the whole display goes red, StandBy-style: every pixel's brightness is mapped into red so the screen never lights the room. Night is per device, decided by sunrise and sunset from the weather feed, and can be switched off per screen or paused with \"Not tonight\" until the next sunrise.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Pure black ground (#000), no card chrome on the board.",
      "One iOS dark system tint per widget, mapped by widget type, never mixed inside a widget.",
      "Heavy rounded numerals (weight 900) as big as the half allows; system sans for words.",
      "Controls hidden until a tap.",
      "Off-board screens are faithful iOS dark UI.",
      "Night turns everything red through a color matrix, not a second palette."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Tint Rule",
        "body": "A widget uses one tint. Its figure, glyph disc and accent marks all read the widget's tint; secondary text is gray, never a second hue. Exceptions are semantic: stock up/down pills (green/red) and the white colon dots.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Night Is a Filter Rule",
        "body": "Night mode is a brightness-to-red color matrix over the whole page (`?night=1` / `?night=0` forces it). Never author red-variant styles per component; design in color and let the filter do it. Night runs only on the display, not on the remote.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Blue Means Act Rule",
        "body": "Off the board, blue marks the action or the selection. There is one filled blue primary button per screen.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Rounded Is for Numbers Rule",
        "body": "The rounded face is for figures: times, temperatures, counts, the login wordmark. Words, including dates written out, use the system sans.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "body": "All interface text is sentence case. Lowercase server strings are capitalized on their first letter by style; nothing is set in all caps or tracked out.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Glass Not Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Anything that floats over content (control pill, tab bar, status badge) is a translucent material with backdrop blur. A shadow is never what separates it; at most a small soft lift where a badge sits on artwork.",
        "section": "elevation"
